BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:CFAR Health Access SWG Webinar with Dr. Russell Brewer
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261007T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261007T190000
DTSTAMP:20260913T061709Z
UID:c0af5815646a5849@gid-seminars.wnprc.wisc.edu
CATEGORIES:Fred Hutch Center for AIDS Research (CFAR)
DESCRIPTION:Event interval: Single day event\nOnline Meeting Link: https:/
 /washington.zoom.us/meeting/register/yYrrRSnOQACbkGzySPUUww\nAccessibility
  Contact: g0201@uw.edu\nEvent Types: Lectures/Seminars\n\nSpeaker: Russell
  Brewer\nTalk Title: Building Responsive Academic-Community Partnerships f
 or Integrated HIV Service Delivery: Lessons from Chicago’s STRIVE Initia
 tive\nBio: Russell Brewer\, DrPH\, is a Research Professor in the Departme
 nt of Medicine at the University of Chicago (UC) and Director of Community
 -Engaged Research\, Capacity Building\, and Southern Initiatives at the Ch
 icago Center for HIV Elimination (CCHE). He also serves as Co-Director of 
 the Behavioral\, Social\, and Implementation Sciences Core of the Third Co
 ast Center for AIDS Research (TC-CFAR). For more than two decades\, Dr. Br
 ewer has led public health research and practice initiatives across academ
 ic institutions\, nonprofit organizations\, governmental public health age
 ncies\, and philanthropy. His expertise spans community-engaged and implem
 entation research\, organizational capacity building\, program development
  and implementation\, and mixed-methods evaluation. Since 2022\, Dr. Brewe
 r has worked closely with frontline community-based organizations in Chica
 go to strengthen organizational infrastructure and build capacity to imple
 ment and sustain integrated HIV\, mental health\, substance use\, and othe
 r wraparound services. He also has a longstanding program of research focu
 sed on communities impacted by the criminal legal system in Chicago and th
 e Southern United States. His work brings together correctional systems\, 
 community-based organizations\, public health partners\, and other key sta
 keholders to identify and address barriers to care\, strengthen cross-sect
